/// Sup. Ct. R. 30.1 (Rules adopted December 5, 2022, effective January 1,
/// 2023), as in every edition since 1995: computing "any period of time prescribed or allowed by these
/// Rules, by order of the Court, or by an applicable statute" (a cert
/// petition's 90 days under 28 U.S.C. § 2101(c) and Rule 13.1), the
/// trigger day is excluded and the last day included, "unless it is a
/// Saturday, Sunday, federal legal holiday listed in 5 U. S. C. § 6103, or
/// day on which the Court building is closed by order of the Court or the
/// Chief Justice", when the period runs to the next day that is none of
/// them. UNVERIFIED: whether Inauguration Day, which § 6103(c) lists for
/// employees in the District of Columbia area, is "listed in § 6103"; the
/// § 6103(a) holidays alone give the earlier date. No closure order is
/// vendored, so none is declared. Service adds no time: Rule 29.3 serves
/// "personally, by mail, or by third-party commercial carrier", and Rule 30
/// adds nothing for any of them.
counting sup-ct-30 effective [2023-01-01, 2026-03-16) known_at 2022-12-05 supersedes sup-ct-30@2019-07-01 cite "Sup. Ct. R. (2023) 30.1"
{
  exclude_trigger_day
  include_last_day
  roll_forward_on [weekend, legal_holiday]
  service_extension {
    Personal: 0.days
    Electronic: unrecognized
    Mail: 0.days
    Clerk: unrecognized
    OtherConsented: unrecognized
    CommercialCarrier: 0.days
    PrisonMail: unrecognized
    MailForeign: unrecognized
    Express: unrecognized
    ExpressForeign: unrecognized
  } cite "Sup. Ct. R. (2023) 29.3"
  holidays us-federal-holidays
  forums [scotus]
}

